a five-hour drive north of sudan's  capital khartoum leads you to one of  this massive country's epicenters of a  multi-billion dollar gold sector  but bloomberg has found through  interviews with local miners government  officials and experts much of that  wealth leaves the country informally  smuggled across borders by land or air  or extracted by informal miners is  packed into bags and ferried in battered  trucks to depots where laborers smash it  up with hammers and deposit it into  rudimentary milling machines  the remaining tailings are transported  to commercial plants many of which have  close ties to sudan's armed forces for  further processing  some of the output is trucked several  hundred miles to the sudanese capital  where it changes hands in a dank  six-storey building downtown  men melt rough nuggets into bars cooling  the molten metal in large tubs of water  and dealers can be seen exiting the  premises carrying piles of cash  according to sudan's finance ministry  official output in the country  represents only a fifth of what was  mined meaning that 80 of gold exports go  unregistered and leave the country  informally  sudan is not the only country struggling  with large-scale gold smuggling  reporting carried out by bloomberg  across africa shows the scale of the  smuggling spanning some dozen countries  and involving tons of gold spirited over  borders in operations often overseen by  armed groups or soldiers according to  one united nations expert illegal  exports from east africa alone amount to  billions every year  its main destination is dubai whose  processing firms stand accused of  facilitating the illicit trade by  closing its eyes to imports from dubious  sources  united arab emirates strenuously denies  any involvement in illegal practices but  as global scrutiny over corporate  governance intensifies the extent of the  smuggling now underway poses  increasingly awkward questions for dubai  and its reputation as a gold trading hub  [